# NAIA in-container reverse proxy.
#
# Why this exists: NAIA gates its privileged actions on the request coming from
# the machine the app runs on. The HTTP gate requires BOTH the peer address and
# the Host header to be loopback (app/backend/server/install_manager_routes.py:47),
# and the WebSocket gate looks at the peer address alone
# (app/backend/server/websocket_session.py:294). Under Docker's bridge network the
# peer address is the network gateway (172.x.0.1), so token entry, the tag archive
# download and extension install all answer 403 even for the person at the host.
#
# Caddy runs inside the container and dials the app over 127.0.0.1, so the app
# sees a real loopback peer, and the Host header is pinned to match.
#
# IMPORTANT — where the trust boundary now lives: to the app, every request that
# arrives through this proxy looks local. NAIA's own local/remote distinction is
# therefore not doing any work here, and the WebSocket gate (NAI token entry,
# account settings, cloudflared control) has no second factor to fall back on.
# The boundary moves to Docker's port publishing instead: docker-compose.yml binds
# the published port to the host's own loopback, so nothing off-box can open this
# listener at all. Publishing it on 0.0.0.0 grants admin to anyone who can route
# to the port — put real authentication in front of it before doing that.

{
	admin off
	auto_https off
	persist_config off
	log {
		output stderr
		format console
		level ERROR
	}
}

:{$NAIA_PROXY_PORT:7243} {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{$NAIA_APP_PORT:17243} {
		header_up Host 127.0.0.1

		# Stream responses straight through. Generation and archive downloads
		# report progress over long-lived connections; buffering stalls them.
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
